{number: 23}
{title: It Came Upon the Midnight Clear}
{key: C}

{start_of_verse: 1}
[C] It came upon the [F] midnight clear,
That [C] glorious song of [G] old,
From [C] angels bending [F] near the earth,
To [C] touch their [G7] harps of [C] gold:
"Peace [E7] on the earth, good-[Am] will to men,
From [G] heaven's all gracious [D7] King": [G]
The [C] world in solemn [F] stillness lay,
To [C] hear the [G7] angels [C] sing.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
Still [C] through the cloven [F] skies they come
With [C] peaceful wings un-[G] furled,
And [C] still their heavenly [F] music floats
O'er [C] all the [G7] weary [C] world;
A-[E7] bove its sad and [Am] lowly plains,
They [G] bend on hovering [D7] wing, [G]
And [C] ever o'er its [F] Babel sounds
The [C] blessed [G7] angels [C] sing.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
And [C] ye, beneath life's [F] crushing load,
Whose [C] forms are bending [G] low,
Who [C] toil along the [F] climbing way
With [C] painful [G7] steps and [C] slow,
Look [E7] now! for glad and [Am] golden hours
Come [G] swiftly on the [D7] wing; [G]
O [C] rest beside the [F] weary road,
And [C] hear the [G7] angels [C] sing.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
For [C] lo, the days are [F] hastening on,
By [C] prophet bards fore-[G] told,
When [C] with the ever-[F] circling years
Comes [C] round the [G7] age of [C] gold;
When [E7] peace shall over [Am] all the earth
Its [G] ancient splendors [D7] fling, [G]
And [C] the whole world give [F] back the song
Which [C] now the [G7] angels [C] sing.
{end_of_verse}
